OTA-03           Unbounded SBT Identity List Causes Linear Gas Growth In
                 Permission Checks




 Category                  Severity            Location                                                     Status


 Denial of Service             Medium          core/TSVRegistryV1.sol: 269~284, 729~737                         Pending


  Description


setSbtConfig     appends a newly registered SBT    tokenId   to identityTokenIds    . The array has no upper bound and no
removal path. Even if the identity is later assigned NONE permissions, the  tokenId   remains in the array.


TSVRegistryV1.sol


   269        function setSbtConfig(uint256 tokenId, PermissionFlag permissions, bytes32
   reasonCode)
   270            external
   271            onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE)
   272        {
   273            _requireReasonCode(reasonCode);
   274            if (participantSbt == address(0)) revert SbtNotConfigured();
   275            try ISmartWalletSbtV1(participantSbt).verify(address(this), tokenId)
  returns (bool) {}
   276            catch {
   277                revert InvalidConfig();
   278            }
   279            if (!sbtIdentityConfig[tokenId].registered) identityTokenIds.push(
  tokenId);
   280            sbtIdentityConfig[tokenId] = SbtIdentityConfig(true, permissions);


Permission queries iterate over every historically registered identityTokenIds   entry. Each iteration performs an external
call to ISmartWalletSbtV1(participantSbt).verify(owner, tokenId)         . This is more expensive than a normal storage or
memory loop, and the cost depends on the SBT implementation. The loop also does not stop after finding a valid credential
because it aggregates permissions across all identities held by the user.


